{
  "requirement": "Saved configuration MUST remain distinct from resolved runtime configuration.\nSerializing and deserializing a valid configuration MUST preserve its semantic\nvalues, including explicit versus omitted settings. Optional fields MAY be\nomitted on serialization; defaults and environment-derived values MUST NOT be\nmaterialized into omitted fields. TOML comments and formatting need not\nround-trip.\n\nLoading configuration or running a configured feature MUST NOT rewrite the user\nor project configuration as a side effect. Resolved credentials and live runtime\nobjects MUST NOT enter the serialized data structures. Explicitly supplied\ncredential values are permitted only in the fields documented by the authoring\nfeature; credentials resolved from a profile or environment MUST NOT be written\nback to configuration.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the requirement is met.** The implementation is complete, tests cover the main design space, and the one marking quibble below doesn't change that.\n\n## Implementation (`src/config.rs`)\n\n**Saved versus resolved configuration**\n- The serialized types are `UserConfig`, `WorkspaceConfig`, `UserAuthoringConfig`, `WorkspaceAuthoringConfig`, `AuthoringConfig`, `BackendConfig`, `BedrockConfig`, `OllamaConfig` and `EvidenceConfig`. They derive `Serialize` and `Deserialize`, with `deny_unknown_fields` on the table structs.\n- `ResolvedAuthoringConfig` is a separate type that does not derive `Serialize`. It carries only the selected saved values plus their source path and project root.\n- `resolve_authoring_from` and `resolve_authoring_with_workspace_from` copy the saved `concurrency` and `backend` verbatim. They materialize no defaults and no environment-derived values.\n\n**Round trip and omitted fields**\n- Optional fields use `#[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = ...)]`. This covers `default`, `concurrency`, `endpoint`, `api_key_env`, `api_key`, `aws_profile`, `region`, `credentials`, `session_token`, and the empty `projects`, `options`, `allow` and `disallow` maps.\n- Defaults such as the Ollama endpoint and the effective concurrency are computed only in accessors: `endpoint()` and `effective_concurrency()`. They are never written back into fields.\n- `to_toml` and `parse` exist on both `UserConfig` and `WorkspaceConfig`. `to_toml` validates, then calls `toml::to_string_pretty`. `parse` validates after decoding. Comments are not preserved, which the requirement allows.\n- A user config's `[authoring]` table must carry `configs`, but it may be empty, so a present-but-empty `configs = {}` survives the round trip. This matches `requirements.authoring.configuration_serialization`.\n- Empty `projects`, `options` and `allow`/`disallow` are indistinguishable from omitted ones, as that requirement allows. A workspace `configs = {}` likewise reads back as omitted.\n\n**No rewriting as a side effect**\n- `load_user_config`, `load_workspace_config`, `workspace_document` and the `resolve_*` functions only read.\n- A search of `src/` found no non-test caller of `to_toml` and no code that writes to `habeas.toml` or the user config.\n\n**Credentials**\n- Explicit secrets exist only as `SecretString`, in the documented `credentials.*` and `api_key` fields.\n- `api_key_from` and `api_key` resolve `api_key_env` into a plain `String` returned to the caller. That value is never stored in a serializable structure.\n- `api_key_env` serializes as the variable name only.\n- Bedrock profile and SDK-resolved credentials are never read into the config types.\n\n**Marking**\n- The functions that enforce the behavior carry `{impl}` markers for this requirement: `parse`, `to_toml`, `api_key`, `api_key_from`, the loaders and the resolvers.\n- The data-model structs and their serde attributes are marked with `requirements.authoring.configuration_serialization`, not directly with this requirement. That authoring requirement's text says serialization \"MUST follow the common rules in `requirements.configuration.serialization`\", so it carries out this part, and `to_toml` is the marked entry point. This is a minor marking quibble, not a gap.\n\n## Tests (`tests/configuration.rs`)\n\n- `backend_configuration_round_trips_without_materializing_defaults`: a round trip over Bedrock and Ollama configs, with nested options. It asserts that the serialized text has no `endpoint` or `concurrency`, and that `parse(to_toml(c)) == c`.\n- `authoring_concurrency_defaults_overrides_and_invalid_values`: omitted concurrency stays omitted after serialization. An explicit `concurrency = 7` is preserved and round-trips. Invalid values are rejected.\n- `absent_and_minimal_configuration_are_distinct_and_read_only`: an absent file gives `None` and is not created. A minimal file with a comment is byte-for-byte unchanged after loading, and it round-trips.\n- `workspace_discovery_uses_nearest_file_without_merging_or_rewriting_it`: the workspace file is unchanged after loading. Omitted `configs` stays omitted in the serialized output, and the config round-trips.\n- `ollama_authentication_resolves_separately_and_rejects_missing_values`: the env-resolved key `private-value` is not in the serialized output, while the variable name `MODEL_KEY` is.\n- `explicit_credentials_round_trip_but_diagnostics_and_debug_redact_them`: explicit credentials round-trip. Debug output and errors redact them, and invalid forms are rejected.\n- `user_and_workspace_authoring_tables_round_trip_every_map`: round trips over `projects`, an empty `configs`, workspace `configs` with options, and `ModelRef`.\n- `workspace_defaults_and_configs_combine_with_existing_user_selections`: resolution leaves the saved configuration structures unchanged.\n\nThese cover explicit versus omitted settings, no default or env materialization, read-only loading, and credential handling, including failure cases.\n\n## Residual gaps\n- There is no test of an integer-valued versus float-valued option (for example `2.0`) across a round trip.\n- There is no test that running a configured feature leaves the config file bytes unchanged. Reviewing the code shows nothing writes the files, so this is minor.\n\nNeither gap is significant enough to fail the requirement.",
  "references": "8568e11156882e5f9fa3ff681455163831d68d1a4f6816b022e994dbd51f1dea",
